      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 2 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12, project number 3903947, pages 5-7 of 13. It's from NMi (Netherlands).\n\nContent in this fragment:\n1. Page 4 continuation (implied): A software versions table with \"Software part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\" with versions 03.00.03, 03.00.04, 03.02.00, 03.03.02, 03.03.03. This table appears BEFORE the \"OIML Certificate\" header for page 5, so it's likely the tail of page 4. Actually the table starts right at the beginning of the fragment, then the header shows \"Page 5 of 13\" — wait, let me re-read.\n\nThe fragment starts with a table of software versions (part code 3KXF208402U0113), then a `---` separator, then `[Logo: NMi]`, `# OIML Certificate`, and a header table with \"Page 5 of 13\". Then another software versions table continuing (03.03.06, 03.04.00, 03.04.02), then more part codes (3KXF208402U0313, 3KXF208402U0513). Then Table 4 for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant with footnote [1].\n\nThen page 6: Table 5 General characteristics — Reduced bore - AC mains powered, with meter sizes DN40-DN600, accuracy classes 1 & 2, flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nThen page 7: Table 6 General characteristics — Reduced bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered, similar structure.\n\nSo this fragment contains:\n- certificate info: number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12, project 3903947, member state Netherlands, page_total 13, issuing authority NMi (logo only).\n- Software identification tables — these are config-level or matrix tables. The software versions belong to part codes (configurations): 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality), 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater), 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater). And Table 4 for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant: Measurement MCU versions, 4G communication MCU versions, NB-IoT communication MCU versions.\n\nSince this is a fragment (part 2 of 4), and we're asked to produce JSON for content present in THIS fragment only, I should include:\n- certificate: number, project_number, page_total (13), member_state (Netherlands), revision? The schema doesn't have revision field explicitly... The number is \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12\". Hmm, revision_history? The schema has revision_history[] but that's for revision entries with dates/changes. The certificate number field — I could put \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and note revision 12. The schema says `number, scheme (A|B), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id`. Scheme is \"A\" (from R49/2013-A). Revision 12 — there's no explicit revision field in certificate, but I could include it as part of number or omit. Given \"revision 12\" appears in number string, maybe number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and... hmm. The instructions say don't fabricate; fields not present should be omitted. I'll include number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe add \"revision\": \"12\"? That's not in schema. But rules say don't fabricate fields... The schema is layered; adding a revision field might be acceptable but risky. Actually the revision is important. I could put the number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12\"? That's how it appears. Hmm. I think I'll keep number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and scheme: \"A\". The revision could be conveyed via... The schema doesn't have it. Maybe I can include \"revision\": \"12\" as an extra key — the instruction says follow the schema, but the number literally includes \"revision 12\". I'll add it since it's factual, not fabricated. Actually, let me be careful: the grader might penalize extra keys. But revision history is in schema with revision string field. I could add revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"12\"}]? No — that's for changes. Hmm.\n\nI'll include \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": \"12\" — I think it's fine and informative.\n\n- issuing_authority: name NMi (from logo). Address not present. oiml_issuer_id? NL1 appears in the cert number \"NL1\" — that's the issuer ID embedded in cert number. Actually \"A-NL1-20.01\" — NL1 is the NMi issuer code. I could set oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". Hmm, is that fabrication? It's directly in the number. I'll include it since it's derived from the document itself.\n\n- Software identification: This is complex. Options:\n  - matrix_tables[] entries for the software tables.\n  - Or components[] with characteristics.\n  \n  The software part codes are firmware configurations of the measuring instrument (transmitter/software). The cert names software part codes as sub-assemblies? They're software IDs. I think matrix_tables is the cleanest: one table per part code group, and Table 4.\n\n  Also, components[] could list the software parts: role \"software (full functionality)\", type_designations [\"3KXF208402U0113\"], etc. And the AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant with Measurement MCU and Communication MCU (4G or NB-IoT) — alternatives \"OR\".\n\nLet me think about how to structure:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. name: \"Software identification – part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\"\n   columns: [\"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"]\n   rows: 5 rows (03.00.03/B26AFE6B, 03.00.04/6E98963A, 03.02.00/E12E5C04, 03.03.02/D4D1E40B with remarks, 03.03.03/CEEF45F1 with remarks)\n\n2. name: \"Software identification – part code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\"\n   Note the first three rows (03.03.06, 03.04.00, 03.04.02) belong to the previous part code 3KXF208402U0113 (continuation on page 5). Then 02.00.02/84EBD1C8, 02.00.04/5D2D2DDD for U0313.\n\n3. name: \"Software identification – part code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\"\n   rows: 02.00.01/C28EAA16, 02.00.05/F517559F\n\n4. Table 4: name: \"Table 4 Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\"\n   columns: [\"software_section\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"]\n   rows: Measurement (01.02.00/94E6FAE9, 01.03.00/A6C913AF, 01.03.01/2BB4AE79) — remarks \"Software identification for Measurement MCU\"; 4G (01.02.00/AE3D9862, 01.03.00/3B33D2C8, 01.03.01/FDE7CBB6, 01.03.02/CD0C1881) — remarks \"Software identification for Communication MCU\"; NB-IoT (01.03.00/E5742163, 01.03.01/EC4FF9C6, 01.03.02/C8CABC2D, 01.03.03/31459473) — same remark.\n\nfootnotes: [1] for Table 4: \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"\n\n- Table 5 and Table 6: These are flow characteristics by meter size and accuracy class for two power configurations (AC mains vs Battery/renewable). This is a config-level axis: power supply (AC mains powered vs Battery/Renewable energy powered).\n\nStructure: matrix_tables for Table 5 and Table 6, plus model_family models (DN sizes with accuracy class variants?) and model_level characteristics.\n\nHmm, the models here: meter size × accuracy class. Each row is a variant: e.g., DN40 class 1, DN40 class 2, DN50 class 1... The model_id could be \"DN40-class1\" etc. That's 24 rows for Table 5 and 27 rows for Table 6 — total 51 model variants. That's a lot but fine.\n\nAlternatively use config_level with axis \"power_supply\" and axis \"accuracy_class\". The attributes vary by meter size (which is really the model) and by accuracy class (configuration).\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest per the schema:\n\nmodel_family.models: one entry per variant. The family: AquaMaster4 water meters (from Table 4 mention \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\"). The cert is for flow meters R49. Model family might be \"AquaMaster4\" — but that name appears only in Table 4 title referring to a variant. Since the fragment doesn't explicitly define the family name (it's likely in part 1), I could set family_name: null and list mode