DN500(2), DN600(2) = 24 rows.\n\nTable 6 rows: DN40(1), DN50(1), DN65(2), DN80(2), DN100(2), DN125(2), DN150(1), DN200(1), DN250(2), DN300(2), DN350(2), DN400(2), DN450(2), DN500(2), DN600(2) = 26 rows.\n\nTotal 50 models if fully enumerated. That's a lot of JSON but doable. However, maybe better to keep models as sizes and use model_level with model ids like \"DN40\", and config_level with two axes: power_supply and accuracy_class. But config_level schema has one axis per entry; values have {condition, value}. For attribute q1, axis \"power_supply\": conditions \"AC mains powered\", \"Battery / Renewable energy powered\" — but values also depend on accuracy class and size. Doesn't fit.\n\nGiven constraints, I think the pragmatic solution:\n- matrix_tables: Table 5 and Table 6 in full (rows as objects with numeric values).\n- model_family: family_name null (or \"AquaMaster4\"? The Table 4 title mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" — suggests the instrument family is AquaMaster4. But the variant FEW45Y.Z is one variant. Since part 1 (not shown) likely defines the family, and I'm told to extract only what's in this fragment... \"AquaMaster4\" appears in this fragment. I could set family_name: \"AquaMaster4\". Hmm, risky but it's present in the text. I'll set it since it's directly named.)\n- models: enumerate size+class+power combos? That's 50 entries. Alternatively size+class with power attribute... \n\nLet me reconsider: maybe models should be the meter sizes (DN40-DN600), and model_level characteristics with attribute \"q1\" etc. — but then values differ per accuracy class and power supply, which model_level can't express. So config_level with axis \"accuracy_class and power_supply\"? \n\nActually re-reading rule 5: \"When attributes vary by configuration (not by model — e.g. 2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups; static vs dynamic weighing), use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension.\"\n\nHere the axes: meter size (model), accuracy class (config), power supply (config). The full matrix is attribute × (model × config). The schema allows model_level values to be keyed by model; if I make the model_id encode size+class+power, everything fits into model_level.\n\nGiven the guidance in rule 4's R137 example where each size is a model, here I think the most faithful representation is:\n\nmodel_family.models: 50 entries like {\"model_id\": \"DN40 class 1 (AC mains powered)\"}... that's verbose. Alternatively just list DN sizes as models and put the flow-rate tables in matrix_tables only, plus config_level for the power-supply difference at family level? But the numeric values per size are already in matrix_tables; duplicating all 50×5 values in model_level would triple the output.\n\nHmm. The instructions in rule 4 say for explicit 2-D tables: \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So they want both. The R137 example had only 2 models. Here 50 combos.\n\nCompromise: models = meter sizes × power supply (30 combos: 15 sizes × 2 power), with attributes including accuracy_class varying... no.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and least error-prone. The key data:\n- Table 5: AC mains: size, class, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio.\n- Table 6: Battery/renewable: same.\n\nI'll do:\n- matrix_tables: two entries with full data. columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"], column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. Rows with numeric values (convert commas to dots).\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\" (named in Table 4)... Actually wait — is AquaMaster4 the family? The cert is for \"AquaMaster4\" flow meters presumably; Table 4 says \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\", implying FEW45Y.Z is a specific variant of AquaMaster4. The tables 5/6 cover \"the family of instruments\". So family_name \"AquaMaster4\" is reasonable.\n- models: I'll enumerate p